cache.go:

type SessionCache struct {
    items map[string]*Session
}

func (c *SessionCache) Get(id string) (*Session, bool) {
    s, ok := c.items[id]
    return s, ok
}

func (c *SessionCache) Put(id string, s *Session) {
    c.items[id] = s
}

func (c *SessionCache) Touch(id string) {
    if s, ok := c.Get(id); ok {
        s.LastSeen = time.Now()
        c.Put(id, s)
    }
}

Every login calls Put and every request calls Touch, so several goroutines write the map at the same time. Go maps are not safe for that, and the runtime aborts on purpose. Add a sync.RWMutex:

```go
type SessionCache struct {
    mu    sync.RWMutex
    items map[string]*Session
}

func (c *SessionCache) Get(id string) (*Session, bool) {
    c.mu.RLock()
    defer c.mu.RUnlock()
    s, ok := c.items[id]
    return s, ok
}

func (c *SessionCache) Put(id string, s *Session) {
    c.mu.Lock()
    defer c.mu.Unlock()
    c.items[id] = s
}
```

Touch holds the read lock and then calls Put, which asks for the write lock. The write lock waits until all readers are gone, but this reader is itself waiting for the write lock, so it never leaves: a self-deadlock, and every later RLock queues behind the pending Lock. Do the whole update under one write lock and don't call Put from inside:

```go
func (c *SessionCache) Touch(id string) {
    c.mu.Lock()
    defer c.mu.Unlock()
    if s, ok := c.items[id]; ok {
        s.LastSeen = time.Now()
    }
}
```
The pointer is already in the map, so there is no need to Put it again. Since LastSeen is now only written under the lock, also read it under the lock wherever you check session expiry.

